/**
 * The yearly priors of the power outage and of each storm case in each county, from the outages of EAGLE-I.
 * The import makes the table from the counts, the laws and the reach (`pooledTable`). Thus the table cannot differ from them.
 */
export const EAGLEI_COUNTY_PRIORS: PriorTable = pooledTable({
  head: {"source":"eaglei.outages","asOf":"2026-02-25","scheme":"us-county-fips","method":"perYear is the pooled rate of a county toward the counties of its state: a gamma law of the rates of the counties of the state in the case, and the mean of the rate of the county after its events in its years. A county with few years or few events gets a rate near the mean of its state. A state with no event in a case gets its pooled rate toward the states with events. reach is the mean of the largest count of customers out of each event over the customers of the county. A county with no event gets the plain mean of the reach of the counties of its state with events. In a state with no event, it gets the plain mean of the reach of all counties with events in the case. A county over the limit is in the laws and in the holdout, but not in the table. An event is a run of the 15-minute counts of a county at or above 1% of its customers. A smaller outage is not counted, and that makes a rate too low. One outage that crosses the line more than one time counts more than one time. The years of a county are the calendar years in which the files list it, so a year with no count is not a year of the county. Each of these makes a rate too high. The case infra.power.outage counts the outages in no storm case. Each storm case counts the outages that start in a storm event of the case in the county, from the storm events of NOAA. An outage in the storm events of two cases counts in each case. Thus a sum of the priors of the storm cases counts it two times or more. In the fit, 965 of the 48621 outages in a storm case are in two cases or more. The case infra.power.outage and one storm case do not overlap. The file of the customers gives too few customers to some counties. For 340 counties, the customers are the count that the county reached on 4 different days. A county with no event in any case is not observed, so the prior of its state applies: 76 counties. The laws of the states expect 5.4 such counties, and a county that the files do not see has no event too. A region with more than 12 events at one customer in one year in a case is not in the table, so its prior is no read: 7 regions."},
  units: EAGLEI_COUNTY_COUNTS,
  laws: EAGLEI_COUNTY_POOLING.laws,
  reach: EAGLEI_COUNTY_REACH,
  group: eagleRegion,
  limit: PRIOR_LIMIT,
});
